Actually, if you want writable strings use -fwritable-strings. From my
reading of the gcc manual -ansi doesn't include that

from the gcc manual:
`-fwritable-strings'
     Store string constants in the writable data segment and don't
     uniquize them.  This is for compatibility with old programs which
     assume they can write into string constants.

     Writing into string constants is a very bad idea; "constants"
     should be constant.

Note that -ansi disables // comments, the inline keyword (which you
had trouble with) and other gnu extensions, so it's worth a shot. if
you only want to disable the inline keyword and keep the gnu extension
use -fno-asm (disables asm, typeof and inline)

As mentioned before, do a  make clean  whenever you change any option
that might affect linking.

(This is for gcc version, 3.3.1 BTW)

